pages=read();done=[]
id='def-cyclotomic-klr-quotient-at-a-dominant-weight';done.append(replace(pages,id,r'''Fix the real symmetrizable Cartan datum, finite color set and graded admissible KLR parameters of [[def-quiver-hecke-klr-algebra-for-a-symmetrizable-cartan-datum]] over a degree-zero field $k$. Let $\Lambda$ be dominant, $\lambda_i=\langle h_i,\Lambda\rangle$. For $n\ge1$ choose monic $a_i(u)\in k[u]$ of degree $\lambda_i$ and put $a(x_s)=\sum_{\nu\in I^n}a_{\nu_s}(x_s)e(\nu)$. Define $J_a(\beta)=\sum_{\nu\in I^\beta}R(\beta)a_{\nu_1}(x_1)e(\nu)R(\beta)$ and $R_a^\Lambda(\beta)=R(\beta)/J_a(\beta)$, with $R_a^\Lambda(0)=k$. The direct sum over root degrees has these local units.
The STANDARD choice $a_i(u)=u^{\lambda_i}$ is used throughout the graded categorification, duality-normalization and based statements below; write $R^\Lambda$ for it. Each individual first-strand generator is homogeneous of degree $2d_i\lambda_i$, so this ideal and quotient are graded. The sum $a(x_1)$ need not have one common degree across different colors. Arbitrary monic polynomials with lower coefficients in degree-zero $k$ instead give an UNGRADED deformation; nilpotent dots, homogeneous trace degree and the highest-weight graded $K_0$ identification are not asserted for that branch. Finite dimension, finite root strings, ordinary projectivity and symmetry have the separately stated monic conclusions below. The cyclotomic relation is on the FIRST strand; induction appends on the RIGHT.''',r'''The idempotents isolate the individual generators, so the displayed ideal also equals $R(\beta)a(x_1)R(\beta)$ when the latter expression is restricted to this block. In the standard choice each isolated generator is homogeneous; this proves the grading, without falsely assigning one degree to their sum. Over a degree-zero field a monic homogeneous polynomial of positive dot degree is the pure power. Lower monic coefficients therefore require forgetting the grading. The example $\lambda_i=2$, $a_i=u(u-1)$ gives $k\oplus k$ at root $\alpha_i$, so its two projective classes cannot be the one-dimensional middle weight of $V(2)$; the standard graded hypothesis is substantive. The rank-zero convention avoids an undefined first letter of the empty word. This definition uses no choice.'''))
id='def-cyclotomic-klr-induction-and-restriction-functors';done.append(replace(pages,id,r'''Use the standard graded quotient of [[def-cyclotomic-klr-quotient-at-a-dominant-weight]]. Put $A=R^\Lambda(\beta)$, $B=R^\Lambda(\beta+\alpha_i)$ and $e=e(\beta,i)$. Right append defines a graded CORNER homomorphism $\iota_i:A\to eBe$, sending $1_A$ to $e$, $e(\nu)$ to $e(\nu i)$, and the old dots/crossings to their positions in the first $|\beta|$ strands. It is not asserted injective and $e$ may be zero. Define $F_i^{\rm basic}(M)=Be\otimes_A M$ and $E_i^{\rm basic}(N)=eN$. These additive functors preserve shifts and satisfy $F_i^{\rm basic}\dashv E_i^{\rm basic}$ by degree-zero tensor-Hom evaluation. $F_i$ increases ROOT degree by $\alpha_i$ and decreases module weight $\Lambda-\beta$ by $\alpha_i$; restriction does the reverse. Both composites are endofunctors at a fixed root degree. The same underlying definitions apply ungraded to monic deformations. Exactness/projective preservation and the shifted second adjunction are theorems below.''',r'''Each generic generator relation is preserved in the corner, and the first-strand cyclotomic generator maps into the longer ideal. This gives a homomorphism with unit image $e$, not a global-unit inclusion. For example $\lambda_i=1$, $\beta=\alpha_i$ has $A=k$, $B=0$, so the corner homomorphism and induction are zero. Evaluation at $e$ identifies $\operatorname{HOM}_B(Be,N)$ with $eN$, proving the ordinary adjunction with its actual grading. Finite balanced tensors give additivity/shift compatibility. No unshifted second adjunction is assumed; its explicit shift is supplied by the coherent-adjunction lemma.'''))
id='lem-word-mirror-involution-of-a-klr-algebra-and-the-quantum-star';old=next(i for p in pages for i in p['items'] if i['id']==id);done.append(replace(pages,id,r'''On the generic graded KLR algebra define $\rho(e(\nu))=e(\operatorname{rev}\nu)$, $\rho(x_a)=x_{n+1-a}$, $\rho(\tau_a)=-\tau_{n-a}$. This is a degree-preserving ALGEBRA AUTOMORPHISM with $\rho^2=1$, commuting with the generator-fixing anti-involution $\psi$. It maps $e(\nu)Re(\eta)$ to $e(\operatorname{rev}\nu)Re(\operatorname{rev}\eta)$. Twisting LEFT modules by $a\cdot m=\rho(a)m$ is an equivalence and commutes with finite vector duality and projective Hom duality. Its separated-block formula is $\rho^*\operatorname{Ind}(M,N)\cong\operatorname{Ind}(\rho^*N,\rho^*M)$, without a shift. Under $\gamma:U_A^-\to K_0(R)$ it induces the quantum multiplication-reversing star, fixing every $F_i$ and $q$. The first cyclotomic generator maps to $a_{\nu_1}(x_n)e(\operatorname{rev}\nu)$, with the SAME original first color. Thus it identifies the first- and last-strand quotients; preservation of the first ideal itself is not asserted in general.''',r'''Check the full presentation: quadratic relations use $Q_{ij}(u,v)=Q_{ji}(v,u)$; the two mixed equal-color constants exchange and the crossing minus corrects their sign. The three-crossing braid words exchange positions; their odd crossing sign and reversal of numerator/denominator give the actual native divided-braid correction. All far and dot relations persist. Applying the map twice fixes generators. Since $\psi$ fixes generators and reverses multiplication, $\rho\psi=\psi\rho$; finite-projective evaluation and finite vector duality therefore commute with the twist. Reversing the block embedding reverses both factors AND twists each, giving the displayed induction formula. Consequently the induced $K_0$ action is an anti-automorphism fixing the divided generators, exactly star. Applying $\rho$ to $a_{\nu_1}(x_1)e(\nu)$ gives the stated last generator; the color is not the new first color. These finite calculations are choice-free.'''))
id='def-cyclotomic-klr-diagram-duality-and-graded-module-duals';done.append(replace(pages,id,r'''For the standard graded quotient $B=R^\Lambda(\beta)$, $\psi$ is the descended anti-involution fixing $e(\nu),x_a,\tau_a$. For a finite module $M$ define $D_\psi M=\operatorname{HOM}_k(M,k)^\psi$, with $(af)(m)=f(\psi(a)m)$. For a finite graded PROJECTIVE $P$ define instead $P^\#=\operatorname{HOM}_B(P,B)^\psi$. Both reverse internal shifts and are involutive on their respective domains. They are DIFFERENT dualities; Hom duality is not applied to a nonprojective simple to produce its cover. By [[lem-cyclotomic-projective-covers-and-dualities-descend-through-the-quotient]], each simple orbit has a unique $D_\psi$-self-dual representative $S$, and its unique normalized cover $P$ with $\operatorname{Hom}^0(P,S)=k$ is $\#$-self-dual. These are the normalizations in the based statements. No self-dual shift is asserted for every indecomposable finite module. The symmetric theorem additionally gives $D_\psi P\cong P^\#\{-2\operatorname{def}_\Lambda(\beta)\}$; it does not change the canonical projective normalization.''',r'''The cyclotomic ideal is $\psi$-stable because its individual first-strand polynomial generators are fixed. Finite vector-space evaluation proves the double $D_\psi$ identity. Evaluation on shifted free modules, then finite retracts, proves the double projective Hom-dual identity. Quotient Hom basechange carries generic normalized projective covers to their nonzero normalized cyclotomic covers, and inflation carries the normalized vector-dual simples unchanged. The quotient-cover lemma proves the normalizations and the unscaled pairing without a Frobenius premise. The trace theorem relates the two dualities only afterward. In rank one $B=k[x]/x^\lambda$, $B^\#=B$ but $D_\psi B=B\{-2d_i(\lambda-1)\}$, illustrating the distinction.''', ['def-cyclotomic-klr-quotient-at-a-dominant-weight','def-graded-ring-module-bimodule-and-internal-shift','def-finitely-generated-graded-projective-module','def-the-klr-cartan-pairing',COVER]))
save(pages,done)
